Verify any of these with:

    python -c "import hashlib,sys;print(hashlib.sha256(open(sys.argv[1],'rb').read()).hexdigest())" matlab/<file>.m

or  sha256sum matlab/<file>.m       (Linux/macOS)
or  Get-FileHash matlab\<file>.m    (PowerShell)

**Where these came from.** They are the COM reference implementation as
circulated by the IEEE 802.3 COM ad hoc. This repository does not record the
retrieval URL or date; if you need to establish that you hold the same file, the
SHA-256 above is the authoritative check, and it is what a future upstream
release should be diffed against.


2. Derived material inside this repository
------------------------------------------

Three kinds of file are derived from the upstream above and carry its licence:

  octave/com_ieee8023_4p15p0_octave_compat.m
  octave/com_ieee8023_4p16p0_octave_compat.m
      The reference made to run under GNU Octave: 4p15p0 from the
      adaptive-local-search build (the primary reference), 4p16p0 from the
      release. Generated by octave/make_octave_compat.py from matlab/ with the patch set in
      octave/patches/: five subfunctions replaced, two added, nine line edits
      (eight by exact text, one by pattern), every one a no-op under MATLAB. Each file names its source and its
      source's SHA-256 in a provenance block after the licence header. Three of
      the replaced bodies (CDF_ev, COM_CommandLine_Parse, writecsv_transposed)
      and csvread4com come from Rich Mellitz's Octave_compat branch of the COM
      repository (Copyright 2025 802-COM Authors). The touchstone reader and the
      floating-tap search are ours, derived from the upstream functions they
      replace and carrying the upstream licence.

  octave/patches/*.m                   (7 files)
      Those bodies as the generator consumes them, licence headers intact.
      com_octave_accel_on.m is the exception: it decides whether the optional
      compiled kernels are used and is original work (Copyright 2026 Todd
      Bermensolo, BSD-3-Clause), not derived from upstream.

  octave/accel/com_octave_accel.cc
      The optional compiled kernels: three of the release's hottest loops
      translated to C++, operation for operation, so that they return what the
      interpreted code returns. A translation of upstream functions, as the
      py_impl.py files are, so it carries both copyright notices. Optional and
      never committed as a binary; octave/accel/build_accel.py builds it.

  com_functions/fn/*/py_impl.py        (157 files)
      The Python translation, function by function. Each corresponds to a named
      MATLAB function and a line range recorded in com_functions/registry.json.

  com_functions/fn/*/matlab_source.m   (49 files)
      Verbatim excerpts of the upstream MATLAB, kept beside their translations
      so a reviewer can compare them without opening the full reference. These
      are literal copies of BSD-3-Clause material, Copyright 2025 802-COM
      Authors, and are covered by LICENSE.

  sicopr.py
      Generated from the py_impl.py files by assemble_sicopr.py. Carries both
      copyright notices and the SPDX identifier in its header.


3. Correspondence to the upstream
---------------------------------

com_functions/registry.json maps every translated function to the upstream lines
it derives from — 157 entries, each carrying "matlab_lines": [start, end]. That
is the machine-readable record; tools/matlab_version_diff.py uses it to map an
upstream diff onto the translations needing re-check.


4. What is NOT covered by the upstream licence
----------------------------------------------

The BSD-3-Clause grant on the MATLAB source does not extend to the channel
S-parameters, configuration workbooks or MATLAB result workbooks used to
correlate this port. Those are IEEE 802.3 contributions with their own terms and
are not redistributed here. See README section 1.


